            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is reasonably air tight, with a few annoying air leakage areas that will identified together during the blower door test. These current air leakage locations include:



** Around the current wood stove insert



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The rear door weather-stripping



** An intentional hole is the 5" chimney for the current furnace (that could be eliminated with a furnace upgrade)



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 26 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.1 GJ. As mentioned above, your house is already reasonably air tight, so I do want to ensure your house has adequate, controlled ventilation. Please read the Ventilation Sections of this report.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the maon floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x4 (R12) and a previous owner had added 3/4" (R3) of expanded polystyrene insulation when the house was resided and had newer windows installed.



The current exterior of this house is in pretty good shape, so you are likely not looking to complete another exterior renovation ............. however as you &amp; Lynn will be considering this upgrade for #12 Polar Creasc, I thought I would at least model adding more exterior wall insulation to this house as well.



I have modeled the addition of another R20 (eg. 4" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls - in addition to the current 3/4" (R3) of expanded polystyrene that is already there.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R20, you could potentially save $ 318 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 50 USG electric hot water tank. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a Uniform Energy Factor of 0.91 or higher.



Although I quite like using electricity for heating &amp; hot water in "hydro electric" communities of the NWT (as there are no associated GHG's), I am often asked to illustrate the estimated cost of alternative fuels - as the homeowner looks for cost savings. Most Fort Smith residents are already using an alternative fuel for their primary heating appliance (oil or propane furnace or boiler), so performing this optional calculation for your hot water use is often requested. 



Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing propane-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.91 UEF, could potentially save $ 762 per year in energy costs and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.7% efficient oil furnace.  As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option you may want to consider when you are ready for a future furnace replacement. Have a look at an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 192 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



Yes ....... I realize the estimated fuel savings of only $192 sounds very low. This is because fuel prices tend to have alot of fluctuation and fuel oil prices were quite low (compared to propane) when we completed our most recent Fuel Cost Library in December or 2020 (fuel rates for Fort Smith are summarized later in this report). If I used our previous 2019 fuel cost rates, the annual savings above would have been $870. The Arctic Energy Alliance is not promoting fuels based on their costs - we try to consider a balance between fuel costs, efficiency of the heating equipment available and the overall GHG reductions that can be achieved.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms for DHERP, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan - does not vent to the outside

        - 1 bathroom fan on the main floor

        - A clothes dryer



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switche (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. As your house could use a little more ventilation, you could even install an automatic timer switch to turn this bathroom fan on/off a few times per day for 20-30 minutes.



As outlined in the previous Air Sealing section of this report, your house is reasonably air tight and is expected to become tighter if you proceed with the upgrade recommendations in this report. If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a "full house" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Almost all houses from the 60's &amp; 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating.



As you continue with your "Renovate Tight" upgrades, you will also want to ensure you "Ventilate Right". I think you can achieve adequate ventilation if you replace the main floor bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit (outlined above).



If you ever notice excess condensation building up on the main floor windows, you may need to have a 5" fresh air duct brought into the return air plenum of the furnace. This way, each time the furnace (or the bathroom fan or clothes dryer) operates, a little bit of outside fresh air is drawn into &amp; distributed throughout the house using the furnace supply ductwork. That fresh air supply pipe should have an internal damper that you can manually adjust between fully open &amp; fully closed. This way you can open it a bit further as condensation forms on windows (or the indoor air quality seems stale with odours) or close it further if there are no window condensation issues and the inside of the house feels dry with alot of static electricity.



Savings: I mentioned in the air sealing section of this report there would be about $25 of annual savings by eliminating the uncontrolled air leakage locations. Adding some additional "controlled" ventilation (outlined above), is estimated to cost about $25 annually. You may look at this and say "Why bother, just leave everything as it is". The problem is that the ventilation created by the air leakage into walls, ceilings &amp; attics creates moisture related damage to your house over many years ........... sometimes creating significant mould &amp; rotting in the walls &amp; ceiling of the house. It is always better to eliminate the "bad" leakage and replace it with "controlled" ventilation (ie: an Energy Star HRV or other exhaust fans + a dedicated fresh air supply into the furnace plenum). Your occupancy will be more enjoyable &amp; you will have more control over the amount of ventilation the house (and the occupants) receives.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the clothes dryer - the kitchen range hood only recirculates air inside the house), indicated a pressure drop of only 4.64 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is chimney vented and could be susceptible to back drafting if the negative pressure created in the house was greater than 5 Pa. I believe the current wood stove insert has a dedicated outside combustion air supply.



Your current furnace draws its' combustion air from inside the house. The furnace upgrade option outlined previously would no longer require a chimney &amp; would draw its' combustion air from outside of the house. The Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends that Wood &amp; Pellet Stoves have outside dedicated combustion air supply pipes installed that are directly connected to the back of the stove. 



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



